.lua
book.chapter(11, "Tuples, lists, and persistent data")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Products and sequences solve different problems"
.pp
A tuple holds a fixed number of components whose types may differ. A list
holds a variable number of elements with one element type. These are different
contracts even when a particular list happens to contain exactly two values.
A point represented as an integer pair guarantees two coordinates; a list of
points permits any number of such pairs. Choose the representation according
to which dimensions are fixed by the domain and which vary with the input.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[swap_pair (x, y) := (y, x)
distance_squared (x, y) := x * x + y * y
point := (3, 4)
Test.equal "tuple shape" (swap_pair point) (4, 3)
Test.equal "tuple computation" (distance_squared point) 25
Test.equal "list of products" (List.map distance_squared [(3, 4), (1, 2)]) [25, 5]]=],
    "A product inside a sequence")
.endlua
.pp
Parentheses also group expressions, so (x) is just x. Unit () is a distinct
primitive value, not a one-element tuple. Tuple patterns unpack values in a
function parameter or match branch. Core.fst and Core.snd are convenient for
pairs, but descriptive patterns usually read better than repeated projections
through a deeply nested product. Records become attractive when positions
would otherwise need extensive comments to explain their roles.
.sh 2 "Curried and tupled interfaces"
.pp
A pair-taking function and a curried two-argument function are not the same
interface. Core.curry converts the former to the latter, while Core.uncurry
goes in the other direction. These adapters are useful when a library produces
pairs but your existing operation is curried. They preserve the computation
while making the input structure explicit to the type checker.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[pair_sum (x, y) := x + y
curried := Core.curry pair_sum
tupled := Core.uncurry curried
Test.equal "curry" (curried 20 22) 42
Test.equal "uncurry" (tupled (10, 5)) 15
Test.equal "map pairs" (List.map tupled [(1, 2), (3, 4)]) [3, 7]]=],
    "Adapt a function to a collection's element shape")
.endlua
.pp
Remember that f(x, y) is supported as a multiargument call. Whitespace
application to an explicit tuple, as in tupled (10, 5), is clearer when
documenting a product-taking function. A public API should not require its
reader to guess whether a comma describes call arguments or a value whose
shape belongs to the domain model.
.sh 2 "Lists share tails"
.pp
The empty list is [], a literal uses brackets and commas, and x :: xs
constructs a head with tail xs. Lists are immutable singly linked sequences.
Constructing a new head can share the old tail because no language operation
mutates its cells. Two lists can therefore have different prefixes and the
same suffix without copying the entire suffix. This is the central advantage
of persistent data for functional transformations.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[tail := [2, 3]
first := 1 :: tail
other := 9 :: tail
Test.equal "first prefix" first [1, 2, 3]
Test.equal "second prefix" other [9, 2, 3]
Test.equal "unchanged tail" tail [2, 3]
Test.equal "concatenation" (first @ [4, 5]) [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]]=],
    "Persistent construction and concatenation")
book.graph({
    {id="A", label="first: 1", x=0, y=0.7},
    {id="B", label="other: 9", x=0, y=-0.7},
    {id="T", label="shared tail: [2, 3]", x=2.7, y=0}
}, {{"A", "T"}, {"B", "T"}}, "Two prefixes can share one immutable tail")
.endlua
.pp
Concatenation with @ must preserve the left sequence before reaching the right
one, so repeatedly appending singleton lists to a growing result is expensive.
Use cons with a reversed accumulator and reverse once, or use a standard
traversal that already follows an appropriate strategy. Sharing does not make
all operations constant-time. Indexed access still walks a linked sequence,
and retaining a small prefix may retain the reachable suffix as well.
.sh 2 "Ranges have two contracts"
.pp
The library function List.range start stop is half-open: it includes start
and excludes stop. The syntax [start .. stop] is inclusive. Both return empty
lists for descending bounds. This distinction is easy to overlook when
rewriting an algorithm, and it produces classic off-by-one errors. Use the
library form for index intervals and spell out the endpoint convention in
public documentation.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[Test.equal "half-open" (List.range 2 5) [2, 3, 4]
Test.equal "inclusive" [2 .. 5] [2, 3, 4, 5]
Test.equal "empty interval" (List.range 5 5) []
Test.equal "descending" (List.range 5 2) []
Test.equal "safe head" (List.head []) None
Test.equal "safe index" (List.nth 1 [10, 20, 30]) (Some 20)]=],
    "Endpoints and optional queries")
.endlua
.pp
Oversized ascending range requests produce an invalid-operation diagnostic;
the implementation checks distances without signed-overflow arithmetic.
That guard does not mean every representable request is affordable in your
application. Materialized ranges allocate lists. When a loop can carry a
counter instead, there is no need to allocate all indices merely to consume
them immediately.
.sh 2 "Choose the right access pattern"
.pp
List.head, tail, last, and nth use Option for absent results. A nonempty value
must still be unpacked before using its payload. Repeated nth calls in a loop
can turn a simple traversal into quadratic work. Prefer a recursive cons
pattern, List.map, List.fold_left, or List.enumerate depending on whether you
need transformation, aggregation, or indices alongside elements. The operation
should match the representation rather than simulate an array API badly.
.pp
Structural equality works when the elements themselves are comparable. A list
of functions is a legal value but not generally a legal target for generic
comparison at runtime. A list of foreign handles compares identities rather
than recursively examining external resources. If the application needs
semantic equality, define a projection or comparator that states which parts
of the domain matter.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read metalib/list/mod.ml and the runtime's list construction and traversal
helpers. Implement zip by matching two lists and stopping at the shorter one,
then compare it with List.zip on empty and unequal-length inputs. Estimate the
cost of an algorithm that calls nth for every index. Finally, rewrite a loop
that repeatedly appends one element so that it uses a reversed accumulator;
verify both result order and the empty-input case.
